{"qid":"Q1986139","names":["Parthian Empire"],"title":"Parthian Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Parthian Empire, founded c. 247 BC when Arsaces I led the Parni tribe in seizing the satrapy of Parthia from Seleucid control, became the dominant power of the Iranian plateau and Mesopotamia for nearly five centuries. At its height under Mithridates I (r. 171–138 BC), the empire stretched from the Euphrates to eastern Iran, and under Mithridates II (r. 124–91 BC) it controlled trade routes between Rome and China, earning recognition as a great power. Parthia repelled Roman invasions decisively, most famously annihilating Crassus's army at Carrhae in 53 BC, but internal dynastic strife and repeated Roman incursions eroded its strength. The Arsacid dynasty fell in 224 AD when the Sasanian vassal Ardashir I defeated and killed the last Arsacid king Artabanus IV, founding the Sasanian Empire.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is partially attested through Parthian administrative records, royal inscriptions, and Greek/Roman accounts, but exact provincial boundaries — especially on eastern and northern frontiers — remain conjectural and subject to ongoing scholarly revision.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Parthia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Parthia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Parthian Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parthian_Empire"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Parthian Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Arsaces I","nativeName":"𐭀𐭓𐭔𐭊","roman":"Aršak","start":-247,"end":-217,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":n